A||||10||RZURCTN||Changing the color of the dash lights?||||5||
Z||000000||RZURCTN||01-18-2000||09:58 PM||||While taking apart an extra dash pod today, I noticed that the plate in front of the guages was painted orange(presumably to give the speedo & tach  that nice red glow).  So what happens if I paint it white?  Will the speedo & tach then glow a soft white in the nighttime?  Or what about a nice neon blue?  If it is the reflection of the color that determines what the guage lights look like then where do the possibilities end?  Or am I just a fool?||||reg||5
Z||000001||87 Steve GT||01-18-2000||10:07 PM||||Hey.... Good Idea.... Sound's like it makes sense to me...  =).. Let Us know what you do...  Steve....||||reg||10
Z||000002||RZURCTN||01-19-2000||11:04 PM||||Well...........one drawback.  I consider it minor, is that there is a slight reflection that shows on the guages.  Other than that, it looks pretty good to me!  And it didn't cost me a penny!  Maybe I'll get a picture of it and post it some day.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||01-20-2000||07:52 PM||||What colordid you wind up using?  Were you able to match up the center pod ligthing? I would love to go green to match my stereo illumination. What do you mean by the reflection?  And have you seen the white guage faces on ebay?  That might help out as well.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||01-20-2000||08:36 PM||||I think that some of that flouresent type marking paint would be the stuff to use. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||01-20-2000||08:59 PM||||A guy on the fiero-list a while back experimented with this.  He said that he could reproduce red and green rather well, but blue left something to be desired.  Of course individual results may vary.  He was by the way using the florescent paint (if I remember correctly).  If you are wondering... no I didn't go this route.  I prefer backlit gages.  I have ordered my white face gages from Super X though.  They will cost me more than nothing, but in the end I think that they will be worth it.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000007||stimpy||01-21-2000||12:26 AM||||William, please let us know how the white face guages work with the backlit guages. I am a little confused on this. Are early dashes different from later models as far as illumination go? Aren't the guage faces overlays? Are they tranlucent or opaque? I'm sooo confused!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||01-21-2000||06:31 AM||||The 84 and 85 gauge clusters are lit by reflected light.  The light bulbs are behind the gauge and reflect off the flourescent orange paint on the insides of the housing.  The gauge faces are solid.  The 86 and later (I believe it starts in 86) use a translucent gage face that the light shines through.<P>I have an 85SE v-6.  I picked up a set oralley gauges and the instrument cluster pod from an 86SE v-6 and plan to try to install them in my car.  I'll post results when I finally get around to the project.<P>By the way I believe al 4 cyl gauges have white tick marks on the speedo and tach while the v-6 has orange (or maybe it is the other way around).  I only get to see my car on Saturdays when I go home for the weekend.  I've been on an out of town assignment for 21 months.  This made it tough to do an engine swap and new clutch and a new A/C compressor install.  I've got one more week than I get to go home.  YEAHHHH!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||01-21-2000||12:30 PM||||I installed a set of 86 SE (backlighted) gauges, including the center console gauges, in my 88 (non-backlighted) coupe. Was pretty much "plug and play". The only difficulty I had was the tach. There is a capacitor that needs to be changed depending on whether you have a 4 or a 6. (I can find the value, if you need it.)<BR>However... if you have an 87 or 88 4-cyl, with the DIS ignition, it's easier to just install your original circuit board on the new tach. Due to the way the DIS fires the plugs, I found that my RPM readings were exactly TWICE what they should have been.<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||01-21-2000||01:00 PM||||Although it has pretty much been covered, I will try to explain what years/models had what gages.  <P>Front lit gage equipped cars are as follows:  all 84 & 85 Models; 86, 87, 88 Econo Coupes & Sport Coupes, 86 SE's\<P>Back lit gage equipped cars are as follows:  86, 87 & 88 GT's and 88 coupes with "Formula" option.<P>The coupes gage faces used orange ticks, while the GT's and SE's (I think) used white ticks.  <P>Back lit gages are constructed of translucent material covered with an opaqe finish over the majority of the gage.  The only part that remains un finished is then lit by light passing through.  This type of gage is much clearer.<P>The front lit gages simply reflect light back upon the gage faces.  This type often has some glare associated with it.  <P>The gage faces that Super X sells (yes, I have a set) work only with the back-lit gages.  I own an 85 GT and an 87 SC, but have converted them to the back lit design for the clearer look.  Super X's gages are excellent quality, fit and finish.  I have been extrememly happy with his products.  <P>****NOTE****  All above information from the above gages has been acquired from Mark Corbin's "Fiero Spotter's Guide".  It is available through Mark for $4 with a $1 shipping charge.  They can be purchased from sending a $5 check to: <P>Mark Corbin<BR>Fiero Spotter's Guide<BR>5474 S.R. 19<BR>Galion, OH 44833<P>Make checks payable to Mark... No I am not affiliated with Mark in any way, but most of our production # questions can be answered in this one little book.<P>William T. Thorn, III<P>||||reg||10
Z||000011||RZURCTN||01-21-2000||05:15 PM||||I used a flat white spray enamel to cover the flourescent orange( it took a few coats to do it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) It came out pretty decent.  You have to take the Tach apart to paint the back of it too.  It's not difficult and it doesn't take very long.  I think that if you were to use a gloss or semi-gloss that it would cause excessive reflection on the guage face.  My opinion:  I'm happy with it!! <BR>The center pod lighting (ie, the climate control)  is lit via a piece of red tinted plastic behind the control.  I didn't change the color of that.  If you could make a piece of clear plastic to fit, then...whalla!  White lights!  If you wanted to go to a darker color, say blue, the I'd try a bright gloss shade.  As close to flourescent as you can get it.  If you can't find gloss, than you could try to clear-coat it.  I think that because blue is so dark it doesn't reflect as well.  Just my $0.02!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by RZURCTN (edited 01-21-2000).]||||reg||14
